/Title (The United Nations High Commissioner for Refugees, UNHCR, or UN Refugee agency was established in 1950 by the UN General Assembly after the Second World War, and it is guided by the 1951 Refugee Convention. Currently it operates across 122 countries and assists 129.4 million refugees. It is in charge of coordinating international action to protect refugees, forcibly displaced communities and stateless people by providing essential life- saving assistance like shelter, food, water, and medical care. The UNHCR also supports former refugees who have returned to their home country, people displaced within their own country, and individuals who are stateless or whose nationality is disputed.)
